<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tabasco</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tabasco is a state in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, with its capital at Villahermosa. Its low, wet plains are crossed by the Grijalva and Usumacinta rivers. It was home to the Olmec centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a> and the Maya city of Comalcalco, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> landed there in 1519. Its economy rests on oil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and farming.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tabasco.jpg" alt="Tabasco" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elelicht · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Villahermosa_La_Venta_Park.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tabasco, officially the Free and Sovereign State of Tabasco, is one of the 32 federal entities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It covers 24,731 square kilometres and is divided into seventeen municipalities. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> to the northeast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> to the south, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a> department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> lies to the southeast. The northwest lies on the coastal plain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Rain falls plentifully all year, and most of the state is covered in rainforest. Since 1994 it has been divided for documentation into two regions named after the Grijalva and Usumacinta rivers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec civilisation</a> dominated much of what is now Tabasco about 3,000 years ago, and its main site there is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>, surrounded by swamps and marshes about 15 km from the Gulf. The Maya began to dominate part of the state around 300 CE; their sites include Comalcalco, Pomoná, El Tortuguero and Jonuta. At Comalcalco they built in adobe brick because the area lacked building stone, using clay and lime extracted from shells.</p><p>The first Spanish contact came in 1518, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva">Juan de Grijalva</a> landed at the mouth of the river that now bears his name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> came the next year and fought the natives at the Battle of Centla; among the twenty female slaves he received as conqueror was La Malinche. The territory was never fully subjugated because of its climate, terrain and lack of minerals, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> who arrived in the 16th century did not stay. European diseases decimated the local population, and the Spanish brought African slaves to work plantations. Significant agricultural production, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, came only in the 18th century.</p><p>Tabasco became one of the first fourteen states under the 1824 Constitution. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Matthew C. Perry</a> took the port of Frontera in 1846 and later Villahermosa, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups and the climate forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to withdraw. Its forces returned in 1847 and occupied the capital for thirty-five days, causing great destruction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Villahermosa is the capital. The state produces petroleum and natural gas, and its farming includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, coconut, bananas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Most speakers of indigenous languages use the Tabascan dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chontal-maya">Chontal Maya</a>. The Parque Museo de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a> in Villahermosa, created in the 1950s by the writer Carlos Pellicer, shelters major pieces from La Venta, and Comalcalco is the Maya site open to the public. The state seal was granted in 1598 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> to the town of Villahermosa, then called San Juan Bautista.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tabasco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Landa reports that the Cocom brought into the city soldiers from the garrisons of <b>Tabasco</b> and Xicalango on the Gulf Coast, the Ah Canul, and says that the Maya of Yucatán learned from them the use of the bow, the lance and the axe, of shields and of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Guatemala, a formal plaza with ceremonial platforms was laid out around 1000 BCE, and at Aguada Fénix in <b>Tabasco</b>, revealed by an airborne laser survey and published in 2020, people raised between about 1000 and 800 BCE an earthen platform some 1,400 metres long and 400 metres wide,…</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Olmec heartland lies in the hot, wet lowlands along the southern Gulf of Mexico, in southern Veracruz and western <b>Tabasco</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/olmecs">Olmecs · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Cortés's expedition of about 500 men reached the <b>Tabasco</b> coast.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-13">13 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Comalcalco grew in the alluvial lowlands of <b>Tabasco</b>, close to the Gulf Coast, where there was no building stone.</blockquote><a href="/state/comalcalco">Comalcalco</a></li><li><blockquote>La Venta rose on a small island in the Tonalá river marshes of <b>Tabasco</b> from about 900 BCE, as San Lorenzo declined.</blockquote><a href="/state/la-venta">La Venta</a></li></ul></section>
